==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|with}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|withe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wiþþe|t=band, tie}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wiþiz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*wiþjǭ|t=cord, rope}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*wéh₁itis|t=that which twines}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*weyt-|t=that which winds or bends, branch, switch}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wey-|t=to turn, wind, bend}}. 
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|da|vidje|t=wicker}}, {{cog|is|viðja|t=willow}}, {{cog|sv|vidja|t=withe}}, {{cog|fr|guiche|t=strap; kiss-curl}}, {{cog|it|guiggia|t=armstrap; sandal strap}}, {{cog|fi|vitja|t=chain; ponyfoot}}, {{m|fi|vitsa|t=birch; twig, wicker}}, {{cog|izh|vitsa|t=branch, twig}}, {{cog|liv|vitsā|t=twig; strap}}, {{cog|vep|vic|t=twig}}, {{cog|vot|vittsõ|t=cane; twig; wire}}; also {{cog|ga|féith|t=calm, still; smooth; bashful, shy}}, {{cog|gv|feagh|t=calm, docile, still}}, {{cog|gd|fèath|t=calm}}, {{cog|cy|gwden|t=withe; snare; noose}}, {{cog|la|vītis|t=vine}}, {{cog|el|ιτιά|t=willow}}, {{cog|prg|witwan|t=willow}}, {{cog|be|ве́йка|t=eyelash}}, {{cog|cs|větev|t=branch}}, {{cog|zlw-opl|wiećw|t=vine branch}}, {{cog|ru|ветвь|t=branch, bough, limb}}, {{cog|sk|vetva|t=branch}}, {{cog|sl|veja|t=branch}}, {{cog|uk|віта|t=branch, twig}}, {{cog|hy|գի|t=juniper tree; cypress}}, {{m|hy| գիհի|t=juniper tree}}, {{cog|ckb|بی|t=willow}}, {{cog|kmr|bî|t=willow; poplar}}, {{cog|ps|وله|tr=wala|t=willow}}, {{cog|fa|بید|tr=bēd / bid|t=willow}}, {{cog|sa|वेत्र|t=large tree; cane}}. {{doublet|en|vice#Etymology 2|t1=a type of tool, etc.}}. 
{{col-bottom}}
The brickwork and chimney architecture senses may have a different etymology, see {{m|en|wythe}}.

